🔍 How the System Works
1. Dataset Collection

The project uses the:

CIC-IDS-2017 Dataset

The dataset contains network-flow information representing normal and malicious network activity.

2. Data Preprocessing

The raw CSV files are processed using Python and Pandas.

The preprocessing stage includes:

Reading CSV files
Combining required data
Cleaning the data
Handling invalid values
Handling missing values
Preparing data for Machine Learning
3. Training Dataset Creation

A smaller training dataset was created to make Machine Learning training more practical on hardware with limited RAM.

The training dataset contains approximately:

299,991 rows
79 columns

The 79 columns consist of:

78 network-flow features
1 target label
4. Feature and Label Separation

The Label column is separated from the network-flow features.

The Machine Learning model uses:

78 features

to predict the traffic category.

5. Label Encoding

Text-based attack labels are converted into numerical values using:

LabelEncoder

The encoder is saved using Joblib so that predictions can later be converted back into their original attack names.

6. Train/Test Split

The dataset is divided into:

80% Training Data
20% Testing Data
7. Random Forest Training

A Random Forest Classifier is trained using the prepared network-flow data.

Random Forest was selected because it works well with tabular datasets and can handle a relatively large number of numerical features.

8. Model Evaluation

The trained model is evaluated using:

Accuracy
Precision
Recall
F1-score
Classification Report
9. Model Saving

The trained model and label encoder are saved using Joblib.

models/
├── cyber_threat_model.pkl
└── label_encoder.pkl
🧠 Machine Learning Model
Model Used
Random Forest Classifier

The trained model uses:

78 network-flow features
14 traffic/attack classes

The original training data contained 15 labels.

However, the Heartbleed class had only one sample in the selected training dataset.

Because at least two samples are required for the stratified train/test split, the Heartbleed class was removed during model training.

Therefore, the final trained model contains:

14 classes
